# MCP Ollama Router 0.2.0

Local task routing for code, explanations and documentation. Two entry points:

- **MCP stdio** (`python -m app.server`): a client reads files and delegates
  selected excerpts to the `chat` tool. `route_preview` performs no inference.
- **Chat-completions gateway** (`python -m app.gateway`): Cline can select
  `auto` as its model provider, with native file tools handled by Cline.
  This avoids an obligatory second model call through MCP for every request.

This release is for a single-tenant, localhost company pilot. It is not a
production certification or a guarantee of model answer quality.

## Install

Python 3.11–3.12; Python 3.11.9 on Windows is the validated local environment.

```powershell
py -3.11 -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pip install -e ".[test]" -c constraints-windows-py311.txt
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pip check
```

The constraints file pins the installed Windows/Python 3.11 dependency closure.
For other platforms use `pip install -e ".[test]"` and validate independently.
The old uv.lock was retired because it did not match this release; pip and
pyproject.toml are the installation interface for this pilot.

## Configure

Copy `.env.example` to `.env` without overwriting an existing configuration.
Settings are validated, read once at startup, and use the `ROUTER_` prefix.
Restart processes after changes. Ollama defaults to `http://127.0.0.1:11434`.

For the gateway generate a random API key and set `ROUTER_GATEWAY_API_KEY`:

```powershell
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m app.gateway
```

Cline configuration: OpenAI-compatible provider, base URL
`http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1`, model `auto`, your API key, context 8192 and output
limit at most 2048. Keep Cline file tools enabled. Do not also force every
request through the separate MCP `chat` tool. The gateway implements a subset;
end-to-end behavior must be verified with the installed Cline/model versions.

## Routing and resource policy

| Task | Preference | Default output tokens |
|---|---|---|
| Write/fix code | Code specialist | 768 |
| Explain a concept | Smaller general model | 384 |
| Write documentation | General writing model | 1024 |
| Analyze a problem | Reasoning capability if eligible | 768 |
| Basic conversation | Smaller general model | 192 |

Detailed requests use up to 1536 tokens; brief requests up to 256. Overrides
are bounded by the configured limit (2048 by default). Gateway requests with
tools require a model advertising native tool support and default to 1024
output tokens. The gateway preserves client instructions; MCP chat injects
only the task-specific response template.

Selection uses lightweight Vietnamese/English rules and model metadata, not
an extra classifier LLM call. New models are discovered at startup. Native
capabilities/context are read using `/api/show` when needed, without inference.
The default model size ceiling is 8B, and unknown sizes are excluded. Configure
`ROUTER_MAX_MODEL_BILLIONS` for the test hardware; names such as `8b` do not
override metadata reporting 8.2B. Heuristic routing is not measured quality.

Requests have bounded admission, queue wait, attempts, total deadlines and a
model circuit breaker. MCP turns in one session are serialized and committed
atomically; history is bounded and expires. The gateway is stateless: the
client supplies conversation history. Prompt/answer text and raw session IDs
are not written to application logs. JSON events go to stderr.

MCP uses a 4096-token context by default; the agent gateway uses 8192. Token
counts are conservative UTF-8 estimates, not a model tokenizer. Oversized
current input is rejected, not silently reviewed after truncation. Context
may need to be larger for agent tool definitions; larger context consumes
more memory and can increase latency. Keep one process/worker for the pilot.

## Gateway API subset

- `GET /health/live`: process liveness, no credentials or model details.
- `GET /health/ready`: authenticated provider/catalog readiness.
- `GET /v1/models`: authenticated eligible models plus `auto`.
- `POST /v1/chat/completions`: text messages, function tools, tool results,
  streaming SSE, optional usage, temperature/top_p/stop/seed, token limits and
  JSON-object/JSON-schema output format.

Unsupported features are rejected explicitly (including images/audio, n>1,
forced tool_choice and `parallel_tool_calls=false`). Returned `model` is the
actual model. After streaming starts, failures emit an SSE error and do not
switch models. An HTTP 200 stream can therefore still contain an error;
clients must check events and completion status. Code/tool calls are never
executed by this server. No SSO/RBAC/multi-tenant isolation/HA is provided.

## Validation

```powershell
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest -m "not integration"
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m app.evaluate --out evaluation-routing.json
# Opt-in, only with the gateway running:
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m app.evaluate --live --limit 3 --out evaluation-live.json
```

`pytest -m integration` requires live Ollama/models. Unit tests use mocks.
Evaluation uses synthetic prompts, does not execute generated code, and
excludes answers from its report unless `--include-answers` is provided.
Model quality, tool-calling correctness and performance require a separate
company acceptance corpus and the actual target hardware.

See [the company pilot guide](docs/COMPANY_TEST.md) for setup, limitations,
acceptance criteria and troubleshooting. Build a clean source ZIP with
`python scripts/package_source.py --out release.zip`. It excludes local env,
venvs, logs, caches, models and Git history, and contains a SHA-256 manifest.
